> Row operations preserve the equations in exact arithmetic

For a nonzero selected pivot in column k, eliminating a later row i uses

$$
m_{ik}=a_{ik}/a_{kk},\qquad
 a_{ij}\leftarrow a_{ij}-m_{ik}a_{kj}\ (j\ge k),\qquad
 b_i\leftarrow b_i-m_{ik}b_k.
$$
\alttext(The multiplier is the below-pivot coefficient divided by the pivot. Subtract that multiple of the pivot row from both the coefficient row and its right-hand side.)

A row swap and subtraction of a multiple of another row are reversible, so they
preserve the solution set in exact arithmetic. Once U is triangular, solve

$$
x_i=\frac{c_i-\sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1}U_{ij}x_j}{U_{ii}},\qquad i=n-1,\ldots,0.
$$
\alttext(Back substitution starts at the last equation and divides the remaining right-hand side by its diagonal coefficient.)

The [official LAPACK DGETRF specification](https://netlib.org/lapack/explore-html/d3/d6a/dgetrf_8f_source.html)
also uses partial pivoting with row interchanges. Its blocked LU factorization
stores multipliers and tests for exactly zero diagonal entries. Our scalar core
keeps actual reduced coefficients and transformed RHS, uses an absolute cutoff,
and does not return a packed L factor or implement blocked BLAS operations.
A current-to-original row permutation describes equation order, not a permutation
of solution coordinates.

> Rerun the original three-equation system without a second elimination

The original equations are 2x+y-z=8, -3x-y+2z=-11 and -2x+y+2z=-3.
Their exact mathematical solution is (2,3,-1). The old report replayed Gaussian
elimination locally to display U; the new report reads the actual core output.
The diagram keeps decimal values rather than rounding every coefficient to an
integer, which would hide entries such as 0.2 and 0.667.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gaussian_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gaussian_elimination.hpp>
