> Bounded multipliers do not prevent element growth

Selecting the largest absolute pivot in its column gives |m|≤1 in exact
arithmetic. But an updated coefficient may be as large as |a|+|m| |pivot-row entry|,
so the maximum can double at a stage. Repeating this argument gives the classical
partial-pivot growth bound 2^(n-1). The native growth counter measures the largest
absolute stored coefficient, including the input, divided by the input maximum;
it excludes the RHS. Floating rounding and underflow are outside that exact bound.

The native Wilkinson growth family has diagonal 1, strict lower triangle -1,
last column 1 and other entries 0. Equal magnitudes retain the earliest pivot,
so there are no swaps and each multiplier is -1. The last column doubles after
each stage, attaining the bound. This is not the symmetric Wilkinson matrix
used in eigenvalue examples. The construction follows
[Higham and Higham (1989), Large Growth Factors in Gaussian Elimination with Pivoting](https://doi.org/10.1137/0610012),
with an [explicit matrix from the author's own explanation](https://nhigham.com/2020/07/14/what-is-the-growth-factor-for-gaussian-elimination/).
The original error-analysis reference is
[Wilkinson (1961), Error Analysis of Direct Methods of Matrix Inversion](https://doi.org/10.1145/321075.321076).
The worst-case growth is a possibility, not a claim about every input or the error
for every RHS. The selected RHS below can have very small backward error even
when coefficient growth is large.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gaussian_growth.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gaussian_elimination.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 std::vector<double>x,observed,formula;
 std::print("| n | nontrivial row swaps | largest absolute multiplier | measured coefficient growth | exact family growth | normwise infinity backward error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(std::size_t n:std::array<std::size_t,7>{2,4,8,12,16,24,32}){
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::gaussian_elimination_growth_matrix(n);const std::vector<double>b(n,1);const auto s=dm::algorithms::gaussian_elimination_solve(a,b);const auto r=dm::algorithms::gaussian_residual_summary(a,b,s.solution.x);const auto theory=std::ldexp(1.L,int(n-1));
  std::print("| {} | {} | {:.1f} | {:.0Lf} | {:.0Lf} | {:.3Le} |\n",n,s.work.row_swaps,s.max_multiplier,s.growth,theory,r.backward_error);
  x.push_back(double(n));observed.push_back(std::log2(double(s.growth)));formula.push_back(double(n-1));if(s.growth!=theory||s.max_multiplier!=1||s.work.row_swaps!=0)throw std::runtime_error("growth family");
 }
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.lines(x,formula,"Exact n-1",{220,38,38},2).points(x,observed,"Measured log2 growth",{37,99,235},5)
  .legend({"Exact n-1","Measured log2 growth"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}}).title("Partial pivoting can attain exponential coefficient growth")
  .xlab("Matrix order n").ylab("log2 coefficient growth; multipliers stay at magnitude 1").y_limits(0,40).size(1050,520).save_svg("build/gaussian_growth.svg");
}
