> A small residual still needs a conditioning argument

Recompute r=b-Ax in widened arithmetic, using the original coefficients. The
native summary returns infinity norms and the normwise backward-error expression
from the [LAPACK Users' Guide](https://netlib.org/lapack/lug/node81.html):

$$
\eta_\infty=\frac{\lVert b-A\widehat{x}\rVert_\infty}
 {\lVert A\rVert_\infty\lVert\widehat{x}\rVert_\infty+\lVert b\rVert_\infty}.
$$
\alttext(Normwise infinity backward error is the residual infinity norm divided by matrix infinity norm times candidate infinity norm plus RHS infinity norm.)

The all-zero denominator convention is 0/0=0. A small value describes a nearby
system solved by the candidate; it alone does not bound the candidate's distance
from the true solution. That inference also needs conditioning.
For A=diag(1,10^-10) and b=(1,10^-10), the exact solution is (1,1).
The deliberately inaccurate candidate(1,2) has second-coordinate error 1 but only
a 10^-10 residual and a small normwise backward error. The example below contrasts
this candidate with the solver output; it does not silently label a residual as
forward accuracy. This infinity-norm diagnostic differs from the existing
linalg::backward_error(), which uses Frobenius/2-norm units.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gaussian_residual.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gaussian_elimination.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const dm::algorithms::GaussianMatrix a{{1,0},{0,1e-10}};const std::vector<double>b{1,1e-10};
 const auto s=dm::algorithms::gaussian_elimination_solve(a,b);const std::vector<double>bad{1,2};const auto r=dm::algorithms::gaussian_residual_summary(a,b,bad);const auto good=dm::algorithms::gaussian_residual_summary(a,b,s.solution.x);
 std::print("| candidate | coordinates | residual infinity norm | normwise infinity backward error | second-coordinate absolute error |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 std::print("| Native solve | {} | {:.3Le} | {:.3Le} | {:.3f} |\n",s.solution.x,good.residual_inf,good.backward_error,std::fabs(s.solution.x[1]-1));
 std::print("| Deliberately inaccurate | {} | {:.3Le} | {:.3Le} | {:.3f} |\n",bad,r.residual_inf,r.backward_error,std::fabs(bad[1]-1));
 std::print("\nNative pivot acceptance {}; matrix infinity norm {:.1Lf}; candidate infinity norm {:.1Lf}; RHS infinity norm {:.1Lf}.\n",s.solution.solved,r.matrix_inf,r.x_inf,r.b_inf);
 // These quantities have different units: logs display their orders of magnitude explicitly.
 auto logs=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({std::log10(double(r.residual_inf)),std::log10(double(r.backward_error)),std::log10(std::fabs(bad[1]-1))},{"Residual norm","Backward error","Coordinate error"});
 logs.title("A small residual can coexist with a wrong coordinate").xlab("Deliberately inaccurate candidate (1,2)").ylab("log10 magnitude; quantities have different units").y_limits(-12,1).size(1050,520).save_svg("build/gaussian_residual.svg");
 if(r.backward_error>=4e-11L||std::fabs(bad[1]-1)!=1||!s.solution.solved)throw std::runtime_error("conditioning contrast");
}
